    "response": "An all-male panel and the appointment of a candidate with fewer traditional academic qualifications (ABD vs. completed PhD, 2 vs. 6 papers) are understandable reasons to feel frustrated and question the fairness of the process. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what this composition might signify, alternative explanations, and how you should strategically handle it.\n\n---\n\n### 1. What Might an All-Male Panel Signify?\n\n* **Structural Demographics in Economics:** Economics has well-documented gender imbalances, especially at senior ranks (Full and Associate Professors) who typically serve on hiring panels. An all-male committee often simply reflects the senior faculty makeup of the department rather than deliberate exclusion.\n* **Potential Policy or Procedural Breaches:** Many public universities have explicit human resources or Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) policies mandating that interview panels include diversity in gender (and sometimes ethnicity). An all-male panel may indicate:\n  * An exemption was granted due to small departmental size.\n  * A procedural oversight or disregard of standard institutional hiring guidelines.\n* **Risk of Implicit Bias:** Extensive literature in labor economics and higher education demonstrates that homophily and implicit bias can influence hiring. Panels without diverse representation are statistically more prone to subjective evaluations—such as overvaluing vague criteria like \"industry experience\" or \"departmental fit\" over verifiable academic output.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Alternative Factors to Keep in Mind\n\nWhile the gender composition is a valid concern, hiring decisions in academia often pivot on factors that paper qualifications alone do not capture:\n\n* **Subfield & Strategic Fit:** The department may have urgently needed someone to teach a specific applied curriculum (e.g., financial economics, corporate consulting, data science) where industry background was prioritized.\n* **Interview and Job Talk Performance:** Job talks, teaching demonstrations, and interpersonal dynamics during the two interview rounds heavily weigh into final decisions.\n* **Two-Week Silence on Feedback:** A two-week delay in receiving feedback is common at public universities. HR offices often restrict search committees from giving informal feedback until the successful candidate has formally signed the contract and the appointment is fully processed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommended Next Steps\n\n#### A. Investigate University HR Policies (Quietly)\nPublic universities usually publish their recruitment guidelines and search committee rules online. Look up:\n* The institution's code of practice on staff recruitment.\n* Requirements regarding panel composition and mandatory EDI/unconscious bias training for panel members.\n* Freedom of Information (FOI) or public records policies regarding search committee rubrics.\n\n#### B. Follow Up on Your Feedback Request Professionally\nKeep communication focused on professional development and objective criteria rather than panel demographics:\n> *\"Dear [Search Chair/HR Contact], I am following up on my request from [Date] regarding feedback on my interview for the Lecturer in Economics position. As I continue to refine my applications, insights on how the committee evaluated my profile against the departmental needs would be invaluable.\"*\n\nIf they provide a written rubric or report, it will clarify whether their justification aligns with the original job specification.\n\n#### C. Seek Confidential Advice\nBefore making any formal complaint:\n* **Campus Ombudsperson or Union/Staff Association:** Public institutions have independent ombuds offices or faculty associations that provide confidential guidance on whether procedural errors occurred.\n* **Trusted Senior Mentors:** Consult a trusted senior academic outside that institution. The academic economics community is tight-knit, and formal challenges can carry reputational risks unless backed by documented procedural breaches.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nThe all-male panel is a **legitimate red flag regarding institutional practice and culture**, but it is not definitive proof of unlawful discrimination on its own. Your most effective approach is to first determine whether the university violated its own hiring policies, secure objective feedback, and evaluate your options with a union representative or ombudsperson.\n",
